    "media:imagetext": "Page 3\nLetter No. B-83-37\nMorgen\nfree of interference by Japanese aircraft especially with proper radio com\nmunications and reports of raiding aircraft from the lower Yangtze.\nIchang eastward the operation as\nindicated in our cable 143 would be with\nthe two Commodores and one Dolphin.\nThis equipment would be flown by Chinese\nWe have four pilots who are capable of flying this equipment safely.\nThey would operate from Ichang to Hankow and on down river as far as they\ncould safely go.\nIt would be our intention to paint these airplanes green\nand fly low.\nThe Japanese might not agree to permit these planes\nto fly un-\nchallenged but I am inclined to think with proper notice they would be re-\nluctant to shoot them down.\nThis theory would seem to have some merit in\nview of the fact that Eurasia has been operating regularly into Harkow and\nhas recently increased its schedule to a daily operation. I am not sure that\n\u0026 Japanese pilot would respect a Eurasia plan if he harpened to come across\nit in the air and could catch it but it would appear that there has been at\nleast no attempt on the part of the Japanese to interfere with this service\nas it would be a very simple matter for the Japanese to fly a pursuit ship\nto a high altitude in the neighborhood of Hongkong and wait for the Burasia\nplane to leave Hongkong on its daily schedule and then catch the Eurasia\nplane over Chinese territory.\nAs I say this would be a very simple matter\nbecause the Eurasia plane arrives and leaves on a published schedule.\nThe other route proposed to be flown would be from Chungking\nto Kweilin to Hongkong and the Managing Director claims that this route\nhas been approved by the Ministry of Communications.\nIt would be a DC-2\noperation and the only hazzard in my opinion would be that now run by\nEurasia in its operations round about Hongkong.\nThe merit in the plan as outlined in my hastily written per-\nsonal letter to you dispatched by the last flight and the radio 143, is\nthat a method is provided by which we can protect PAA's investment which\notherwise may be a total loss except for such claims as we might have against\nan unfriendly Chinese Government after peace is restored. On the other hand\nif we can find any basis of cooperation which does not involve too much risk\nwould it not be better to reassociate ourselves with the operations of CNAC\nthereby render a service of great value to the Chinese Government and one\nwhich would tend to assure the carrying out of any agreement which we may now\nmake. When the war is over there will still be a lot of China left and in\nthe Yangtsze River and everything south will still be Chinese\nOur Peking line as you know was never \u0026 greet money maker.\nYangtze River division was the real backbone of the Company and the Coastal\nRoute gave promise of being nearly as profitable.\nIn fact the Coastal Route\npassenger and mail revenue at the time we discontinued operations was greater\non a three times a week schedule than the Peking Division had ever been on a\ndaily schedule.\nTherefore we can probably\nassume that following hostilities\nthe best part of CNAC's territory will still be available for operation.\nthe peace when it comes is a Japenese peace end all foreign advisers are\nforced out and we also are forced out then in that evert we will at least have\na valid claim ageinst the Japanese for the value of our investment in Chine.",
